  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "Runaway",
      "title": "One script, everyone suffers",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "A customer’s script has a bug: it calls your API in a tight loop — 5,000 requests a second. The API servers spend almost all their time on it.\n\nReal users’ requests queue behind the flood and time out. Nobody attacked you; one client simply took more than its share.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Shared capacity needs a fairness rule. Without one, the noisiest client wins."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "abuser": "5,000 req/s",
        "served": "61%",
        "apiCpu": "99%",
        "limited": "0/s"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Limit",
      "title": "A rate limiter at the front door",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Put a [[rate limiter]] in the gateway in front of the API. Each client — identified by API key, user id or IP address — gets a budget, say 10 requests per second.\n\nRequests within budget pass through. The rest are rejected immediately with `429 Too Many Requests`, before they cost the API anything.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Rejecting early is cheap: the gateway answers in microseconds, and the API never sees the excess."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Gateway with a rate limiter",
        "~ Script limited to 10 req/s",
        "~ API CPU 99% → 45%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "abuser": "5,000 req/s",
        "served": "99%",
        "apiCpu": "45%",
        "limited": "4,990/s"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Token bucket",
      "title": "How it counts: the token bucket",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "The most common algorithm is the [[token bucket]]. Each client has a bucket holding up to 10 tokens, refilled at 10 per second. Every request takes a token; an empty bucket means 429.\n\nA real user’s bucket stays mostly full (the green dots). The script empties its bucket instantly and stays empty.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Bucket size allows short bursts; the refill rate caps the long-run average. Two numbers, easy to explain."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ A bucket per client"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "abuser": "5,000 req/s",
        "served": "99%",
        "apiCpu": "45%",
        "limited": "4,990/s"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Algorithms",
      "title": "Windows vs buckets",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "A simpler **fixed window** counts requests per calendar minute and resets at :00. Easy — but a client can send a full minute’s budget at 12:00:59 and again at 12:01:00: double the limit in two seconds.\n\nA **sliding window** smooths that edge; a **token bucket** allows controlled bursts. Most API gateways use one of the latter two.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Need a steady outflow instead (e.g. to a fragile partner API)? A leaky bucket queues requests and releases them at a fixed pace."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "abuser": "5,000 req/s",
        "served": "99%",
        "apiCpu": "45%",
        "limited": "4,990/s"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Many gateways",
      "title": "Many gateways, one budget",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "One gateway isn’t enough, so you run several behind a load balancer. If each counts on its own, a client spread across 10 gateways gets 10× its limit.\n\nSo gateways keep the counters in a shared, fast store (often Redis): each request does an atomic increment and checks the total.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "The shared store adds ~1 ms per request. Some systems trade a little accuracy for speed by syncing local counters every second."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Second gateway",
        "+ Shared counter store",
        "- Per-client buckets now live in the store"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "abuser": "5,000 req/s",
        "served": "99%",
        "apiCpu": "45%",
        "limited": "4,990/s"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Back off",
      "title": "Tell clients how to behave",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "A good 429 says when to come back: a `Retry-After: 30` header, plus headers showing the limit and what’s left. Well-behaved clients slow down and retry later.\n\nThe script’s owner fixes their loop. Now it uses its fair share and nothing more.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Publish your limits in the API docs, and offer higher tiers for customers who genuinely need more."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Retry-After and RateLimit headers",
        "~ Script backs off to 10 req/s"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "abuser": "10 req/s",
        "served": "100%",
        "apiCpu": "38%",
        "limited": "0/s"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "gw2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ga2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "g2r": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Tenants",
      "title": "Case: one customer’s 10,000 users",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "You sell your API to companies. One customer runs a nightly import with 10,000 well-behaved users — each under their personal limit, but together using 70% of your capacity. Other customers slow down every night.\n\nPer-user limits can’t see this. Add limits per **tenant** (customer account), sized by their plan, on top of per-user limits.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Layer limits: per user, per tenant, per endpoint (expensive endpoints get lower limits), and a global ceiling."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Per-tenant quota (by plan)",
        "~ Big import capped at its plan’s share"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "served": "99%",
        "apiCpu": "55%",
        "limited": "1,200/s"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "bot-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "api-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "gw-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "gw2": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "ug-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ga-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ga2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "gr": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "g2r": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Shedding",
      "title": "When everyone spikes: shed load",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Rate limits stop one client hogging capacity. But on launch day *everyone* arrives at once and the total is still more than you can serve.\n\nThen [[load shedding]] decides what to drop: background work like prefetching, recommendations and analytics (amber) is rejected first, so logins and checkouts keep working.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Failing some requests on purpose beats failing all of them by accident."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Real users ×5",
        "+ Low-priority requests shed at the gateway",
        "~ Checkouts still 100%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "served": "100%",
        "apiCpu": "82%",
        "limited": "2,000 shed/s"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "bot-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "api-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "gw-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "gw2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ug-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ga-2": {
          "visible": false
